1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,856
Here is a bug in our prototype.

2
00:00:02,176 --> 00:00:06,429
A student has a referral
to an ear, nose and throat specialist,

3
00:00:06,429 --> 00:00:08,725
valid until January twenty-second.

4
00:00:09,045 --> 00:00:11,244
Their parent opens the booking page,

5
00:00:11,244 --> 00:00:14,421
leaves it open past that moment,
and presses submit.

6
00:00:14,741 --> 00:00:19,382
By the agent's reading of the code,
the expired referral is attached anyway,

7
00:00:19,382 --> 00:00:23,168
and the tracker shows the specialist
as booked, never expired.

8
00:00:23,417 --> 00:00:28,857
All 245 existing tests passed;
none of them books after the expiry.

9
00:00:29,177 --> 00:00:33,529
The code even carries a comment saying
expired referrals are left alone.

10
00:00:33,849 --> 00:00:35,300
No person had noticed.

11
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:38,305
On Friday afternoon an agent reported it,

12
00:00:38,305 --> 00:00:42,169
after trying every order of a few actions
in a small world.

13
00:00:42,489 --> 00:00:47,459
This episode is about how an agent can prove
a change is right before anyone looks.

14
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:52,981
Every change to the app's data goes
through one function, called the reducer.

15
00:00:53,301 --> 00:00:57,225
It takes the current state
and a single action, such as book,

16
00:00:57,225 --> 00:01:01,600
confirm, reschedule, cancel or complete,
and returns the next state.

17
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:04,786
The booking page checks the referral
when it draws,

18
00:01:04,786 --> 00:01:07,147
but the booking reaches the reducer later.

19
00:01:07,467 --> 00:01:11,221
So the order of events matters,
and time passing is an event too.

20
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:13,728
A test follows a path someone chose.

21
00:01:14,048 --> 00:01:16,287
The bug lives on a path nobody chose:

22
00:01:16,287 --> 00:01:19,253
first time passes,
then the booking goes through.

23
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:21,932
A test checks the cases someone thought of.

24
00:01:22,252 --> 00:01:24,151
What checks the ones nobody thought of?

25
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,229
Build a small world instead:
two referrals, three specialist slots,

26
00:01:32,229 --> 00:01:34,375
and a clock with just two moments,

27
00:01:34,375 --> 00:01:38,667
right after the referrals are written
and an hour after they expire.

28
00:01:38,987 --> 00:01:42,080
From the starting state there
are thirteen possible moves.

29
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:45,241
Make each one,
then every move from each result,

30
00:01:45,241 --> 00:01:48,437
layer by layer,
merging any state you've already seen.

31
00:01:48,757 --> 00:01:53,628
In this world that is 13,898 states,
reached in about a second,

32
00:01:53,628 --> 00:01:58,421
and every one of them comes
from the real reducer, not a copy.

33
00:01:58,667 --> 00:02:02,742
Four rules are checked at every state
and a fifth on every step,

34
00:02:02,742 --> 00:02:04,653
all written in plain language,

35
00:02:04,653 --> 00:02:08,219
such as one referral backs
at most one live appointment,

36
00:02:08,219 --> 00:02:11,275
and an expired referral
is never newly attached.

37
00:02:11,595 --> 00:02:13,635
Because the search goes layer by layer,

38
00:02:13,635 --> 00:02:17,611
the first broken rule it meets comes
with the shortest story that breaks it.

39
00:02:17,931 --> 00:02:21,429
Here, two steps:
time passes, then book with that referral.

40
00:02:22,767 --> 00:02:24,068
Why did it slip through?

41
00:02:24,388 --> 00:02:27,119
Booking looks only
at the referral's stored status.

42
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:31,108
Expired is never stored;
it is only worked out for the screen.

43
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:34,372
And the page checks when it draws,
not when you submit.

44
00:02:34,692 --> 00:02:37,103
The agent did not change the product code.

45
00:02:37,423 --> 00:02:41,870
It recorded the bug as a known finding
and left the fix to a person:

46
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:46,383
compare the expiry date inside the reducer,
or check again on submit.

47
00:02:46,617 --> 00:02:49,348
The rule was not loosened
to make the check pass.

48
00:02:49,668 --> 00:02:52,271
And the check is built to fail in two ways:

49
00:02:52,271 --> 00:02:56,025
if any new rule breaks,
and if this known bug ever disappears.

50
00:02:56,345 --> 00:02:59,876
That second failure reminds whoever
fixes it to delete the note,

51
00:02:59,876 --> 00:03:02,745
so the note can never hide the bug
if it comes back.

52
00:03:04,083 --> 00:03:06,814
Why would an agent break its own code
on purpose?

53
00:03:10,683 --> 00:03:13,435
Because a check you have never seen
fail proves nothing.

54
00:03:13,755 --> 00:03:15,376
Maybe it can't fail at all.

55
00:03:15,696 --> 00:03:18,982
So the agent broke the reducer five times
on purpose,

56
00:03:18,982 --> 00:03:22,267
in five different ways,
and ran the check after each.

57
00:03:22,587 --> 00:03:26,528
All five were caught,
each with a story of two steps or fewer,

58
00:03:26,528 --> 00:03:28,688
and each breakage was then undone.

59
00:03:29,008 --> 00:03:33,889
It is a hand-made version
of what testers call mutation testing;

60
00:03:33,889 --> 00:03:36,710
the classic paper on it is from 1978.

61
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:39,927
The idea is old and serious.

62
00:03:40,247 --> 00:03:45,087
In 2015 Amazon described
checking its designs this way,

63
00:03:45,087 --> 00:03:49,399
using TLA+,
a language created by Leslie Lamport.

64
00:03:49,719 --> 00:03:52,881
In one database
service it found a data-loss bug

65
00:03:52,881 --> 00:03:56,439
whose shortest story
was thirty-five steps long, a bug

66
00:03:56,439 --> 00:04:00,194
that had passed design
reviews, code reviews and testing.

67
00:04:00,514 --> 00:04:04,499
Ours is tiny by comparison,
two steps in a one-second check,

68
00:04:04,499 --> 00:04:06,113
but it is the same move:

69
00:04:06,113 --> 00:04:09,879
let a machine walk every order
a person would never try.

70
00:04:11,217 --> 00:04:14,716
The second tool drives the app itself,
the way a parent would.

71
00:04:15,036 --> 00:04:16,487
From a saved demo state,

72
00:04:16,487 --> 00:04:20,177
the agent starts its own copy of the app
and its own browser.

73
00:04:20,497 --> 00:04:24,659
Then it clicks through: open the referral,
choose an in-network clinic,

74
00:04:24,659 --> 00:04:27,942
pick a time, submit, wait for pending,
open the tracker.

75
00:04:28,262 --> 00:04:31,643
It finds each control
by its role and accessible name,

76
00:04:31,643 --> 00:04:33,897
the label a screen reader announces,

77
00:04:33,897 --> 00:04:36,966
and waits for the page
to settle after each step.

78
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:41,431
Right means two things at once:
the screen says the specialist is booked,

79
00:04:41,431 --> 00:04:45,605
and the saved data says the referral now
points to that new appointment.

80
00:04:45,925 --> 00:04:48,465
Each run is required to keep evidence:

81
00:04:48,465 --> 00:04:51,941
screenshots, the saved state,
and no browser errors.

82
00:04:52,261 --> 00:04:55,632
When a step fails,
it says which buttons it could see instead.

83
00:04:55,952 --> 00:04:58,982
And when it's done,
it shuts down only what it started.

84
00:05:00,317 --> 00:05:05,004
Both tools came from trying two skills
in a public plugin called pstack,

85
00:05:05,004 --> 00:05:09,106
on Friday afternoon,
in two commits twenty-seven minutes apart.

86
00:05:09,426 --> 00:05:13,579
Anthropic's own advice
for Claude Code says the same thing plainly:

87
00:05:13,579 --> 00:05:17,298
give the agent a check to run,
or you become the test suite.

88
00:05:17,534 --> 00:05:21,448
What waits for you:
choosing the fix for the expired referral,

89
00:05:21,448 --> 00:05:24,669
and trying the app-driving tool
on your own laptop;

90
00:05:24,669 --> 00:05:26,942
so far it has only run in the cloud.

91
00:05:27,262 --> 00:05:29,182
And the pull request is still a draft.

92
00:05:29,502 --> 00:05:30,733
The last episode asked

93
00:05:30,733 --> 00:05:33,363
whether layers can stand
in for a human review.

94
00:05:33,683 --> 00:05:36,845
Here are two more:
a check that tries every order,

95
00:05:36,845 --> 00:05:39,123
and an agent that uses the real app.

96
00:05:40,467 --> 00:05:42,917
So: one tool walks every order of actions

97
00:05:42,917 --> 00:05:47,102
in a small world and hands back
the shortest story that breaks a rule.

98
00:05:47,422 --> 00:05:51,134
The other uses the real app like a parent
and keeps the evidence.

99
00:05:51,454 --> 00:05:54,327
The first was checked
by breaking it on purpose;

100
00:05:54,327 --> 00:05:57,022
the second,
by running every flow end to end.

101
00:05:57,267 --> 00:05:58,376
Three questions.

102
00:05:58,696 --> 00:06:02,110
What does the exhaustive check try
that an ordinary test doesn't?

103
00:06:02,430 --> 00:06:04,328
Why break your own code on purpose?

104
00:06:04,648 --> 00:06:07,230
And why does the check fail
when the bug gets fixed?

105
00:06:12,467 --> 00:06:16,606
Next: before any backend code is written,
how do you choose the backend?

